Author: sixvolts <rigel@sixvolts.org>
Date:   Wed Sep 30 16:10:06 2026 +0000

    cuda: read each MoE expert once for all tokens routed to it (few-token gate/up)
    
    The few-token MoE path (speculative verify, a few decoding sequences) ran the
    per-slot matvecs with one block row per (token, expert slot), so an expert
    chosen by several tokens was read once per token. For the Q4_K gate/up GLU at
    K = 2560 every such block now loads the whole routing (one wave-wide load and a
    ballot); the first pair that picks an expert owns it and applies each weight
    sub-block to every later token routed there, the others exit. No sort, scratch
    or extra kernel, and no per-slot activation copies; each token's arithmetic is
    the per-slot kernel's in the same order (ub3 KLD bit-identical on/off). Scheme
    from the GLM build on rune. 2-6 tokens (n_used * n_tokens <= 64).
    
    3-token verify (llama-bench pp3, kernel trace): gate/up 91.4 -> 80.8 us per
    call and the slot copies gone, few-token MoE time -9%; pp6 121.9 -> 126.9 t/s.
    The same scheme on the K = 640 Q5_1 down projection was slower (96 vs 71 us)
    and is not used. GGML_CUDA_NO_MOE_DEDUP=1 disables it.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DTjwwGh3znQpq4Yoxunx6b

